            <div xml:id="d908">

                <pb facs="0845" n="837" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg837"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/9322: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-03-25T18:00:00+03:00">March 25, 1941—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 10:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>80. For Secretary and Under Secretary. I now learn that at Cyprus conference
                    reported in my 74, March 21, Turks agreed to make fresh overtures to Yugoslavs
                    for conference as to possible joint action in event of attack upon Saloniki
                    which, if latter agreed, Turks would be prepared to regard a “mortal danger”
                    (rather than “casus belli” as attitude reported in my 39, February 24<anchor
                        n="41" xml:id="d908-f1"/> would have warranted expecting); but that in view
                    of Yugoslav crisis reported in Belgrade’s 219, March 21,<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d908-f2"/> Turkish Government suspended action on that undertaking
                    and has only now consented under British pressure to send its Ambassador
                    seemingly futile instructions to act thereon if he finds occasion suitable.</p>
                <p>Repeated Athens, Belgrade.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
